# Java Testing Requirements

## Purpose
Define the minimum evidence expected from Java tests without imposing a specific testing framework.

## Rules

### JTST-001 · Automated unit tests (MUST)
Provide repeatable automated tests for nontrivial business logic, parsers, validations and transformations.
**Verification:** test results in CI.

### JTST-002 · Boundary and failure cases (MUST)
Test valid, invalid and boundary inputs and expected failure behavior, including cleanup of partial work where relevant.
**Verification:** traceability between scenarios and test cases.

### JTST-003 · Reproducibility (MUST)
Tests must not depend on developer-specific paths, uncontrolled external services or execution order. Use controlled fixtures and deterministic inputs where possible.
**Verification:** clean-environment CI runs.

### JTST-004 · Integration testing (MUST when applicable)
Test integration boundaries such as filesystem operations, process execution, configuration parsing, network calls and persistence. Use real integrations or appropriately justified test doubles according to risk.
**Verification:** integration suite and results.

### JTST-005 · Platform testing (MUST when applicable)
Run applicable integration tests across every platform formally declared as supported, or record specific uncovered combinations as exceptions.
**Verification:** platform-test matrix.

### JTST-006 · Regression on defects (MUST)
For each fixed reproducible defect, add a regression test unless a documented exception is approved.
**Verification:** linked defect and test evidence.

### JTST-007 · Coverage evidence (SHOULD)
Publish test coverage as a diagnostic measure. A single coverage percentage is not sufficient proof of correctness; thresholds are set in project quality-gate configuration.
**Verification:** coverage report.

## Tooling
JUnit and other frameworks may be selected per project; this specification does not mandate one.
